introduction:
Lung Carcinoma, commonly known as lung cancer, is the leading cause of cancer-related death worldwide, accounting for 1.8 million deaths per year. Yet, biopsy for diagnosis still faces the challenge of hitting a moving target (a tumor within the lung tissue) and relies on a percutaneous CT-guided needle operated almost entirely by human hands with minimal robotic assistance [1, 2].
In clinical practice, the standard protocol for percutaneous CT-guided needle biopsy involves the clinician walking in and out of the operating room for each needle adjustment, leading to longer procedure times and increased scan radiation exposure for the patient and possibly the clinician or technician.

To address positional errors caused by the patient’s natural breathing cycle, physicians have implemented a breath-holding protocol in which the patient holds a stable lung position while the interventional radiologist inserts the needle toward the target. This protocol, while widely adopted, is often unsuccessful for those most likely to need this procedure: older patients with severe pulmonary diseases that make breath-holding difficult [1, 2, 3, 4].

Several sensing methods have been considered to address tumor tracking during breath cycles. Contact-based devices such as respiratory belts and bellows provide consistent respiratory signals by tracking changes in chest and abdominal expansion [3, 5], but they can cause patient discomfort. Optical markers attached to patients can track 3D chest motions through spatially resolved measurements, although they require external cameras and continuous marker visibility [6]. Contactless approaches such as structured light and time-of-flight cameras can also be used to measure chest surface motion without markers, though their effectiveness is influenced by factors including camera positioning, surface visibility, and the physical constraints around the CT scanner [7, 8, 9].

Current robotics solutions have made strides in assisting with tumor localization and remove the need for a breath-holding protocol.
A robotic system for trans-thoracic puncture of pulmonary nodules based on gated respiratory compensation was developed by Li et al. [10]. Similarly, Wen et al.  [11] used ultrasound imaging to guide needle insertion with respiratory gating. This method removes the need for patient cooperation in breath-holding protocols, but still requires a surgeon to complete the insertion or manually adjust it after the robotic system completes its measurements.

Furthermore, Schreiber et al. [12, 13, 14] and Zhang et al.[15] demonstrated a robotic arm capable of approaching and driving a needle insertion mechanism within the CT bore, eliminating the need to remove the patient from the CT bore for needle insertion. While this system showed potential to reduce operation time and radiation exposure by working directly within the CT bore, it lacks a precise mechanism to compensate for chest movement caused by the patient’s natural breathing cycle, limiting its potential to safely perform biopsy procedures.

In this work, we present an integrated sensing and control system capable of gated needle insertion within the CT bore. This system was developed to integrate into a CT-guided needle biopsy robot [14], addressing current gaps in robot-assisted needle biopsy procedures.
Our contributions include:

A model for quiet breathing;

An integrated sensing and controls mechanism capable of working within the CT bore;

Autonomous four stage linear needle insertion procedure with gated controls;

Anomaly detection during autonomous procedure.

1. A model for quiet breathing;

2. An integrated sensing and controls mechanism capable of working within the CT bore;

3. Autonomous four stage linear needle insertion procedure with gated controls;

4. Anomaly detection during autonomous procedure.
